              "content": "# What is Cantor?\n\n**Cantor's set theory** is a branch of mathematics that deals with infinite sets and their properties. Developed by Georg Cantor in the 19th century, it provides the foundation for modern mathematics and has profound applications in computer science, physics, and biology.\n\n## Key Concepts:\n\n1. **Infinite Sets**: Cantor proved that there are infinitely many different infinite sets, each with its own properties.\n\n2. **Continuum Theory**: The theory of infinite sets is known as \"continuum theory\" and is essential for understanding the structure of infinite sets.\n\n3. **Cardinality**: Cantor's work on cardinality (the measure of the size of a set) is fundamental to understanding the relationship between different infinite sets.\n\n4. **Continuum Hypothesis**: The hypothesis that there is no set of the same cardinality as the set of all natural numbers is a cornerstone of continuum theory.\n\n5. **Applications**:\n   - **Computer Science**: Used in algorithms and data structures\n   - **Physics**: Applied in cosmology and particle physics\n   - **Biology**: Used",
